"Let $G_{{\\mathcal X},p}$ be the randomly embedded random graph on $n$ points in $[0,1]^d$, with edge-probability $p$, and let $T(G_{{\\mathcal X},p})$ denote the length of a minimum tour. Suppose that\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{n^\\alpha}\n$$\n\nfor a constant $0<\\alpha<1$. **Asymptotic tour-length conjecture.** There exists a constant $\\beta_\\alpha^d$ such that, asymptotically almost surely,\n\n$$\nT(G_{{\\mathcal X},p})\\sim \\beta_\\alpha\\frac{n^{\\frac{d-1}{d}}}{p^{1/d}}.\n$$\n\nThe preceding results motivate seeking an asymptotic formula for the minimum tour length, while the conjecture restricts attention to power-law edge probabilities rather than arbitrary decreasing functions $p$."